{
  "text": "I am honored to be with you today at your commencement from one of the finest universities in the world. Truth be told, I never graduated from college. Today I want to tell you three stories from my life. The first story is about connecting the dots. I decided to drop in on a calligraphy class to learn how to do it. I learned about serif and sans serif typefaces, and about what makes great typography great. None of this had even a hope of practical application in my life. Ten years later, when we were designing the first Macintosh computer, it all came back to me. You cannot connect the dots looking forward; you can only connect them looking backward. So you have to trust that the dots will somehow connect in your future. My second story is about love and loss. I was lucky. I found what I loved to do early in life. Woz and I started Apple in my parents garage when I was twenty. In ten years Apple grew from just the two of us into a two billion dollar company. And then I got fired. How can you get fired from a company you started? Getting fired from Apple was the best thing that could have ever happened to me. The heaviness of being successful was replaced by the lightness of being a beginner again. My third story is about death. Remembering that you are going to die is the best way I know to avoid the trap of thinking you have something to lose. You are already naked. There is no reason not to follow your heart. Stay Hungry. Stay Foolish.",
